.TH JETBRAINS-TOOLBOX-INSTALLER 1 "September 2026" "jetbrains-toolbox-installer 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
jetbrains-toolbox-installer \- verified official JetBrains Toolbox App delivery
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer install --assumeyes
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer update --assumeyes
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer release --json
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer prepare --directory
.I DIR
.RB [ --app-version " BUILD"]
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer apply --archive
.I PATH
.B --sha256
.I HEX
.B --app-version
.I BUILD
.B --assumeyes
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer status --json
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer uninstall --assumeyes
.br
.B jetbrains-toolbox-installer --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
This helper targets Fedora 44 x86_64. The helper RPM contains no application
payload. Installing, reinstalling or upgrading the RPM automatically queues a
one-shot systemd job that downloads and installs the package-selected JetBrains
Toolbox release. No second installer command is required. DNF completion
reports queuing, not application readiness. Check systemctl status
jetbrains-toolbox-installer.service and journalctl -u
jetbrains-toolbox-installer.service for completion or failures.
The job waits for the DNF transaction and retries failed operations at
60-second intervals, limited to three starts in 15 minutes. Reinstalling the
helper queues a fresh attempt after a persistent problem is fixed.
Application updates arrive through new helper RPM release selections.
Final DNF package removal stops the job and its retries, then removes the
verified application bundle and desktop integration. Upgrades and reinstalls
do not uninstall the app. A stop, ownership or cleanup failure prevents erasing
the helper, allowing a later retry. Installed IDEs, projects and settings under
the home directory are retained.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install, update
Require root and --assumeyes. Reconcile the release selected by the signed
helper package. Verify existing ownership before network access. A current,
verified installation is unchanged; missing known links can be repaired
offline. Refuse downgrades, changed release metadata and conflicting ownership.
Download the exact official archive and apply it using the same verification
and ownership rules as prepare/apply. Print installed-state JSON.
.TP
.B release
Print the package-selected build, source and SHA-256 as JSON, offline.
.TP
.B prepare
Run without root. Download the latest stable official Linux archive, or the
build selected with --app-version, into a private child of an existing
directory. Verify its exact HTTPS release URL, the SHA-256 published beside it,
expected size and gzip identity. Print metadata JSON with schema_version, name,
arch, version, sha256, path and source. The caller owns successful-download
cleanup and must show the selected artifact before approval. Failure removes
the incomplete download. These checks are not a publisher signature.
.TP
.B apply
Require root and --assumeyes. Work offline. Copy the selected archive into
private privileged staging, verify it again, refuse downgrades and unpack it
without executing anything it contains. Only directories, regular files and
internal relative symlinks are accepted. Verify owned files before replacement.
Publish the bundle and install the fixed integration links. Print verified
installed-state JSON. The caller must approve the exact build and SHA-256
before invoking this command.
.TP
.B status
Read-only and offline. Print JSON containing schema_version, name, installed,
verified, integrated and cleanup_pending, plus the approved identity when
installed. Invalid or foreign state is an error, never successful absence.
Missing known integration links are repairable through approved apply.
.TP
.B uninstall
Require root and --assumeyes. Verify ownership before removing integration
links and the application bundle. A durable removal journal permits resuming
interrupted removal. Foreign or modified surviving entries block cleanup.
Preserve home directories, installed IDEs, projects and Toolbox settings.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /opt/jetbrains-toolbox/
Owned archive, unpacked app, launcher, desktop entry and inventory receipt.
.TP
.B /usr/local/bin/jetbrains-toolbox
Owned symlink to the launch wrapper.
.TP
.B /usr/local/share/applications/jetbrains-toolbox.desktop
Owned symlink to the desktop entry.
.TP
.B /opt/.jetbrains-toolbox-removal.json
Durable ownership journal for interrupted removal.
.TP
.B /opt/.jetbrains-toolbox-removing
Owned pending-removal bundle.
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/JetBrains/Toolbox/.settings.json
Current user's Toolbox settings, defaulting to
~/.local/share/JetBrains/Toolbox/.settings.json. The launcher creates this file
with autostart disabled when it does not exist yet, and never changes an
existing file. Toolbox owns it afterwards.
.SH NOTES
Toolbox enables autostart by default and rewrites the XDG autostart entry from
its own settings on every launch. The launcher records the opt-out before the
first launch, so the app does not start with the session unless the user
enables that in Toolbox settings. Starting the unpacked executable directly,
bypassing the launcher, skips this step.
Toolbox cannot update itself inside the root-owned bundle; application updates
arrive through new helper packages. The one-shot job runs on package changes,
not periodically or at every boot. Bundle exchange is atomic; the complete
install or removal transaction is not. Missing known links and interrupted
removal can be retried, while unknown ownership or modified files require
inspection. Empty parent directories remain after removal. DNF removal invokes
the verified uninstall operation automatically; standalone uninstall remains
available for app-only removal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Nonzero indicates invalid input, unsupported platform,
unverified ownership or a failed operation. SIGTERM and keyboard interruption
return nonzero. A failed operation must not receive a successful caller receipt.
